Bi-Microporous Metal-Organic Frameworks with Cubane $[M_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ 1 $[M=M]$ , Co) Clusters and Pore Space Partition for Electrocatalytic Methanol Oxidation Reaction
Direct methanol fuel cells (DMFCs), with high energy conversion efficiency and low operating temperature, have attracted extensive attention as a green carbon-neutral technology to meet the growing energy demand[1]. However, the anode kinetics of methanol oxidation reaction (MOR) is sluggish, creating an essential need for better  electrocatalysts to enhance the performance of DMFCs[2]. Although great progress has been made towards the development of non-precious-metal-based catalysts, Pd- or Pt-based materials are still well-recognized high-efficiency electrocatalysts for methanol electro-oxidation. But the high cost and limited supply of platinum and palladium deter the large-scale commercial development of DMFCs[3]. One key issue for DMFCs technology is to fabricate cost-effective and high-efficiency electrocatalysts for anodic methanol oxidation reaction [4]. Therefore, the search for efficient, durable and inexpensive alternatives to Pt-based catalysts is of utmost importance. Although Ni-based nanomaterials[5] such as NiO[6], $N i(O H)_{2}[7]$ , Ni-LDH[8] and PtNi[9] and ${\mathsf{N i C u}}^{[10]}$ alloy  particles have been studied as promising candidates for electrochemical MOR, the tendency for nanoparticles to aggregate and the deactivation by harmful intermediates are some factors limiting their applications.
Metal−organic frameworks(MOFs), with their unique properties and large surface areas, have been extensively explored as promising electrocatalysts[11]. Specially, the integration of diverse active units such as titanium oxide clusters [12], zirconium oxygen clusters[13], polyoxometalates[14], transition metal oxygen clusters[15] into MOFs can remarkably enhance their activities for catalysis. As one of important metal cluster SBUs, tetranuclear clusters such as square-planar, $Z n_{4}O$ -type, and cubane-type have drawn extensive interests. The classical square-planar $M_{4}$ units[16] and $Z n_{4}O.$ -types[17] are very common. In comparison, MOFs based on cubane $[M_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ cluster are rare. Recently, clusters such as $[\mathsf{C o}_{4}\mathsf{O}_{4}]^{[18]}$ , $[\mathsf{M n C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}]^{[19]}$ , $[\mathsf{M n_{4}C a O_{4}}]^{[20]}$ , $[(\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{F e})_{4}\mathsf{O}_{4}]^{[21]}$ have been used as catalysts for photosynthesis and electrocatalytic water splitting. Although various structural mimics of the electrocatalysts have been reported [22] , cubane $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ cluster-based porous MOFs as an anode electrocatalyst for MOR have been rarely studied.
Similar to the well-known 6-connected $[Z n_{4}0]^{6+}$ cluster found in well-known MOFs such as MOF-5, $[\mathsf{N i_{4}(O H)_{4}}]^{4+}$ also have six symmetrically equivalent edges defined by two adjacent metal sites at the vertices of the $M_{4}$ metal tetrahedron. Thus, in principle, new $[\mathsf{N i_{4}(O H)_{4}}]^{4+}$ structural chemistry can be created by re-imagining the existing library of $[Z n_{4}\mathsf{O}]^{6+}$ chemistry. A challenge is that while both $[Z\mathsf{n}_{4}\mathsf{O}]^{6+}$ and $[\mathsf{N i_{4}(O H)_{4}}]^{4+}$ can act as 6-connected nodes, $[\mathsf{N i_{4}(O H)_{4}}]^{4+}$ does have a lower positive charge than $[Z n_{4}{\cal O}]^{6+}$ so that ligands or ligand combinations effective in $[Z n_{4}{\cal O}]^{6+}$ structures may not work with $[\mathsf{N i_{4}(O H)_{4}}]^{4+}$ . To address this challenge, our synthetic strategy explores the use of -3 tricarboxylate ligands with neutral bipyridyl ligands, which ideally solve this issue.
Herein, we have synthesized and structurally characterized a Ni-based highly porous 3D MOF, $[\mathsf{N i}_{4}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}(\mathsf{T A T B})_{4/3}(\mathsf{B P E})_{2}]$ (CTGU-15, TATB $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 4{,}4^{\prime}$ ,4''-s-triazine-2,4,6-tribenzoic acid and BPE=trans-1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)ethylene), containing cubane $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ cluster and -3/0 ligand combination. Green polyhedral-shaped crystals of CTGU-15 were obtained by solvothermal synthesis of $N i(C l O_{4})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O$ with TATB and BPE in the mixed $D M A//H_{2}O$ solution with $\mathsf{H B F}_{4}$ as an additive at $120^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 24h. Employing similar synthetic strategy, a cobalt MOF (denoted CTGU-16) has been obtained (Table $\mathsf{s}\mathsf{1}$ ). Since CTGU-16 is isostructural to CTGU-15, the structure and properties of CTGU-15 are described below.
CTGU-15 can be visualized as the pore-space-partitioned Cu2-BTB based MOF-14 framework, and alternatively as a nitrogen-rich, $N i_{4}(O H)_{4}$ variant of $Z n_{4}O.$ -BTB-2,6-NDC-based DUT-6/MOF-205 framework $(H_{3}B T B$ $\mathbf{\delta}=\mathbf{1},3,5$ -Tris(4- carboxyphenyl)benzene, 2,6-NDC=2,6- naphthalenedicarboxylate[23]. In MOF-14, square-planar Cu2 paddlewheel clusters are joined by BTB into (3,4)-connected interpenetrating $\mathsf{P t}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ -type net. In non-interpenetrated DUT-6 and MOF-205, the $Z n_{4}O$ cluster mimics the $\mathsf{C u}_{2}$ square-planar pattern with 4 of its 6 teterahedral egdes. The remaining two opposite edges of each $Z n_{4}0$ cluster are further bridged to adjacent clusters by dicarboxylate ligands (i.e., 2,6-NDC). In CTGU-15, $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ cluster mimics $Z n_{4}O$ clusters with 4 of its 6 teterahedral egdes. However, due to lower charge on $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ cluster and four available Ni coordination sites, two neutral BPE ligands act in tandem to bridge two adjacent $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ clusters. Therefore, unlike the well-known $p a c s$ system[24] in which the pore-partition agent is a single tpt ligand (tpt $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=\mathbf{\varepsilon}_{2,4,6}$ -Tris(4- pyridyl)-1,3,5-triazine), the pore-partition agent in CTGU-15 consists of a ligand pair (two BPE ligands). The pore space partition has been reported as a powerful tool in property engineering on the $p a c s$ platform which is based on trimeric $M_{3}(O H)/O$ clusters. The extension here into $N i_{4}(O H)_{4}$ cubane clusters and the double-ligand mode represents a major advance. One advantage of the double-ligand pore-spacepartition mode is apparently the high stability, as observed in
CTGU-15, which is particularly desirable for electrocatalytic applications  which often occur under relatively harsh conditions (as compared to gas storage and separation applications). The integration of CTGU-15 and ketjen black (KB) generates a series of MOF-based composites KB&CTGU-15 as effective electrocatalyst for methanol oxidation reaction. The optimal catalyst KB&CTGU-15(1:2) shows a much enhanced efficiency.
The X-ray analysis reveals that CTGU-15 crystallizes in the cubic $P m\mathrm{-}3n$ , showing a $30$ neutral porous framework with cubane $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ as the building unit (Figure S5a). The Ni1 center is six-coordinated by two carboxylate O atoms and one BPE N atoms, and three $\upmu_{3}–O H$ atoms in a distorted octahedral $[N i O_{5}N]$ geometry (Figure S2). It is noteworthy that four symmetry-related Ni1 atoms are bridged by four $\upmu_{3}–O H$ atoms to afford a cubane $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ cluster (Figure 1a, Figure S3).
Interestingly, cubane $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ units  are linked by TATB ligand (Figure $(16)$ to generate a porous net similar to MOF-14 based on 4-connected $\mathsf{C u}_{2}$ and 3-connected BTB ligands (Figure 1c and 1d). As a result, an extended 3D network with 1D nanosized hexagon and quadrangle channels running along the [100] direction was formed (Figure 1e). Simultaneously, the original 1D channels along the crystallographic c axis (with an aperture size of $13.4\times27.3\mathbb{A}^{2}$ and $13.4\times13.4\mathbb{A}^{2})$ (Figure S4) has been embeded by the BPE pore-partitioning ligands with parallel and vertical modes (at an interval of ca. 9.3 Å) into numerous uniform interconnected pore cavities (Figure 1f)[25].  Noteworthy is that although the $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ clusters are known, assembling this cubane $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ cluster into 3D metal-organic frameworks is unusual (Figure S5a). CTGU-15 is a rare example of 3D porous MOFs containing cubane $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ unit. Topologically, if the TATB ligand and the $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ unit are simplified as 3- and 6-c nodes, respectively, then the 3D framework of CTGU-15 exhibits a (3,6)-connected ith-d network with a point symbol of $(5^{12}{\cdot}8^{3})_{3}(5^{3})_{4}$ (Figure 1g, 1h and Figure S5b).The total guestaccessible volume in CTGU-15 are is very high, reaching as much as $79.2\%$ , as calculated by PLATON. Obviously, even after the pore-space partition approach, the pore volume is still larger than that of many other highly microporous MOFs. The high surface area and relatively large pore size (Figure S9) are expected to facilitate the adsorption/diffusion of reaction species and enhance the electrocatalytic activity described below.
The characteristic powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) peaks of CTGU-15 match well with the simulated ones from single crystal X-ray diffraction (Figure S6), confirming its good crystallinity and high purity. To assess the chemical durability of CTGU-15 in electrolyte solution, $100~\mathsf{m g}$ of crystalline sample was immersed in $20~\mathsf{m L}~0.1~\mathsf{M}~\mathsf{K O H}$ at room temperature, the PXRD patterns remain unchanged after $24\ h$ (Figure S6). These results confirm the excellent stability of CTGU-15 in 0.1 M KOH, and promise the utilization of it as MOR electrocatalysts in alkaline medium.
The MOR catalytic activity of CTGU-15 was initially recorded at a scan rate of $50\ \mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}{\cdot}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ in ${\sf N}_{2}$ -purged 0.1 M KOH solution with and without $1.0{\ensuremath{\mathsf{M}}}$ methanol (Figure S11). In order to eliminate the double-layer capacitor effect, all obtained data are normalized to mass activity. It is noted that the anodic current density increases sharply after addition of methanol, revealing the obvious electrochemical response of CTGU-15 for methanol oxidation (Figure S11a). When measured in solutions containing 0.1 M KOH with1.0 M $C H_{3}O H$ at different scan rates, apparently, the current density of CTGU-15 increases with the increase of scan rate(Figure S11b).And the peak current densities are linearly increasing with the square root of the scan rate the sweep rates from 10 to $50\mathsf{m V}\cdot\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ as expected (Figure S11c).
Such a response is attributed to the electrochemical activity of the redox couple over the surface that is enhanced with the speed of reaction.
Apart from catalytic mass activity, methanol concentration is another index to assess MOR performance. So the methanol concentration-dependent CV is performed with CTGU-15 catalyst in 0.1 M KOH with different concentrations of methanol (0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0M $C H_{3}O H_{3}$ . When the concentration of methanol is 2.0 or $3.0\mathrm{~M~}$ , the oxidation peak current density keeps constant, suggesting that the rate-determining step has changed to a reaction-kinetics controlled process (Figure S12, Table S4). As we know, most Ni-based catalysts have been reported using lower concentrations of methanol, ranging from 0.5  to $2.0\mathrm{~M~}$ . For CTGU-15, the mass activity and peak current density of CTGU-15 catalyst in 1.0 M $C H_{3}O H$ is larger than that of other concentrations of methanol (0.5 , 2.0  and $3.0\mathsf{M}$ .
In order to further clarify the factors contributing to the MOR activity of CTGU-15, the CV curves for activating catalysts and MOR in 0.1 M KOH and 0.1 M ${\mathsf{K O H}}{+}1.0$ M $C H_{3}O H$ were investigated for KB&CTGU-15 hybrid samples and pure CTGU-15 (Figure 2a and 2b, Figure S13-S16). As shown in Figure 2a, five electrocatalysts exhibit a typical CV behavior in 0.1M KOH solution at $50\mathrm{\mV\}\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ . The peaks are anodic and cathodic at around $0.55~\mathsf{V}$ and $0.45~\mathsf{V}$ , respectively, corresponding to the conversion of Ni(II) to Ni(III) during the catalyst activation. And the peaks centered at around $0.8\mathsf{V}$ are undoubtedly attributed to MOR. Judging from the mass-specific current density in the forward scan, the KB&CTGU-15 (1:2) $(527\ \mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{m g}^{-1}),$ ) exhibits the highest MOR activity, which is larger than those of KB&CTGU-15(1:1) $(456\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ , KB&CTGU-15(1:3)(495 mA·mg−1) and KB& CTGU-15(1:4) $(483\ m{\mathsf{A}}{\cdot}\mathsf{m g}^{-1})$ , and was around $2.5$ times higher than that of pure CTGU-15 (203 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{g}^{-1}.$ (Figure 2c). And that the peak current density of KB&CTGU-15 (1:2) attains $29.8\mathsf{m A\ c m^{-2}}$ at potential of $0.6~\mathsf{V},$ which is higher than those of other catalysts at the same potential (Table S3).The improved activity exhibits in the KB&CTGU-15(1:2) hybrid suggests smaller activation energy for MOR in the presence of moderate KB. Furthermore, as shown in Figure 2d, there is a linear relationship between the current densities and the square root of scan rates for different catalysts, implying that the oxidation of methanol is determined by its diffusion speed. In order to confirm the difference of MOR performance of the double-ligand PSP material (CTGU-15) and single-ligand PSP material MOFs, a well-known single-ligand pore-space partitioned materials(CPM-33a) was prepared according the literature[26]. The PXRD of the as-synthesized sample matches well with the simulated one (Figure S17). The MOR activities of CPM-33a were evaluated under the same testing environment with CTGU-15, the mass-normalized CV and $C H_{3}O H$ electro-oxidation curves were shown in Figure S18. CPM-33a sustains mass peak current density of 110 mA mg−1 and specific area activity $(12.5\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2},$ . Apparently, the MOR performance of CTGU-15 is superior to CPM-33a, which might be attributed to more efficient electron transport and electrolyte ingress between cubane $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ unit and higher BET surface area of CTGU-15. Furthermore, a comparison of catalytic properties between CTGU-15 and its hybrid catalysts compared with known MOR catalysts is listed in Table S5.
The morphology and microstructure of KB&CTGU-15(1:2) further studied (Figure 3).The slice structure of hybrid sample can be clearly confirmed by TEM (Figure 3b and d). Energy dispersive spectroscopies mapping (EDS) elements shows the elemental distribution of Ni, C, N and O are distributed uniformly in the composites, demonstrating that ketjen black particles are decorated in the surface of CTGU-15. Also, the survey XPS spectrum suggests that CTGU-15 contains Ni, C, O, and N species (Figure S10).
To gain insight into the electrode kinetics of the studied catalysts for MOR, the charge transfer resistances (Rct) of all catalysts were assessed. Figure 4a presents the EIS Nyquist plots for five catalysts at onset potentials. It was found that the $\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{c t}}$ of the KB&CTGU-15(1:2) (90Ω) sample was much smaller than those of other composites such as KB&CTGU-15 (1:1) (120 $\Omega)$ ), (KB&CTGU-15 (1:3) (190 Ω)), (KB&CTGU-15 (1:4) $(209\Omega);$ 1 and pure CTGU-15 (358 Ω), which disclose a highly efficient electron transport and favorable MOR kinetics at the KB&CTGU15 (1:2) electrolyte interface.
Finally, chronoamperometry was also used to further evaluate the durability and long-term activity of hybrid electrocatalysts for MOR. Figure 4b shows the chronoamperometric curves in $0.1~\mathsf{M}$ KOH solutions containing 1.0 M $C H_{3}O H$ at a potential step of $0.6\mathrm{\:V}$ for $2400\ \mathsf{s}$ . Clearly, the current densities sharply dropped at the beginning and then slightly decreased for all four electrodes. At the beginning of the reaction, it is a fast kinetic reaction, so the active sites are free of adsorbed methanol molecules. The adsorption of more methanol molecules decides by the liberation of the electrocatalytic sites via methanol oxidation or of the intermediate species during MOR process. Therefore, the slightly decreased current density might be due primarily to the poisoning of the catalysts. These curves show that composites of CTGU-15 with KB sustain more stable current over time i.e. $57.0\ \%$ for KB&CTGU-15 (1:1), $65.0\ \%$ for KB&CTGU-15 (1:2), $61.0\%$ for KB&CTGU-15 (1:3) and $54.0\ \%$ for KB&CTGU-15 (1:4). As expected, the current density for KB&CTGU-15 (1:2) maintains the highest among these catalysts, which testifies the best catalytic durability of it toward methanol electro-oxidation. This enhanced stability for KB&CTGU-15 (1:2) might be due to the improved electron conduction between the CTGU-15 and KB. Some additional characterizations such as PXRD, XPS, SEM and TEM are testified to the robustness of CTGU-15 after MOR. The PXRD pattern in Figure S19 recorded after MOR indicates the CTGU15 sample is stable. XPS measurements of CTGU-15 before and after the MOR was also assessed (Figures S20). The Ni2p spectra of before and after catalysis sample can clearly be identified by two characteristic peaks with binding energies about 870 and 855eV, respectively. Obviously, there were not significant changes in the Ni2p peaks before and after catalysis, suggesting that a similar chemical and structural environment is retained. Meantime, the SEM measurements indicate that the CTGU-15 catalyst retain its’ morphology during the MOR process (Figure S21). The TEM images obtained from the precatalysis CTGU-15 (Figure S22a and 22b) are similar to the post-catalysis samples (Figure S22d and S22e). The SAED pattern of post-catalysis (Figure S22c) CTGU-15 is very close to that of pre-catalysis (Figure S22f) sample. These results suggest that CTGU-15 retain the nature of the original crystalline state after MOR.
In summary, a new 3D porous material type containing cubane $[M_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ $(M=N i$ , Co) cluster has been prepared and structurally characterized. The electrocatalytic applications for MOR of the Ni-form and its composites were evaluated. The asprepared KB&CTGU-15(1:2) hybrid sample exhibit optimal electrocatalytic MOR activity compared with pure Ni-MOF electrocatalyst. This enhanced performance toward MOR could be attributed to the unique active cubane $[N i_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ unit and possible synergetic effect of ketjen black and Ni-MOF. These results not only establish the composite between microporous Ni-MOF and electrical co-catalyst as an efficient and stable electrocatalyst for methanol oxidation reaction in alkaline media, but also demonstrate that the integration of transition-metal cubane $[M_{4}(O H)_{4}]$ cluster into MOFs is a viable method to improve their properties.
